  "_id": "6a7f5797757ee51246381c5c",
  "lab": "6a7f5796eda1a5feaff0b458",
  "name": "Herpes Simplex Virus (HSV) 1-IgM",
  "code": "HSV1IGM",
  "cost": 0,
  "interpretation": "<table><thead><tr><th><div>HSV-1 Test Result</div></th><th><div>Interpretation</div></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><div>IgG Antibodies Positive</div></td><td><div>A positive result for IgG antibodies indicates past exposure to HSV-1. It suggests that the person has been previously infected with HSV-1 and has developed immunity to the virus. The presence of IgG antibodies typically means a history of oral herpes infection.</div></td></tr><tr><td><div>IgM Antibodies Positive</div></td><td><div>A positive result for IgM antibodies suggests a recent or acute HSV-1 infection. It indicates an ongoing or recent oral herpes infection. However, false positive IgM results are possible, and confirmatory testing is necessary to confirm the diagnosis.</div></td></tr><tr><td><div>Both IgG and IgM Antibodies Positive</div></td><td><div>Simultaneous presence of both IgG and IgM antibodies may indicate a recent HSV-1 infection or a reactivation of a previous infection. Further evaluation is required to determine the specific stage and nature of the infection.</div></td></tr><tr><td><div>Both IgG and IgM Antibodies Negative</div></td><td><div>A negative result for both IgG and IgM antibodies indicates no current or previous exposure to HSV-1 and no immunity to the virus. It means the person is susceptible to HSV-1 infection.</div></td></tr></tbody></table>",
